00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.967
- I'm a pastor.
- I'm a pastor.

00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:04.740
- I'm a pastor.
- I'm a pastor.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.180
- I'm a pastor.
- I'm a pastor.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.013
- I'm a pastor.

00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:10.086
(bright organ music continues)

00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:24.150
- So what about an introduction
to start things off?

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.310
My name is Jay, I'm an Episcopal priest.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.220
- I'm Don, a pastor, non-denominational.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.690
- Dominic, Lutheran pastor.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.520
- Paul, a youth pastor, SPC.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.990
- Paul wore a whole suit,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.940
and I was like, oh, maybe
he's overcompensating.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.890
Like, "I don't know what a
pastor would wear on a Saturday,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.030
maybe it's this suit."

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.330
- So I'm Zach, former
Calvary Chapel pastor,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.350
and now like fully...

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.183
- Oh, wait.
- Fully?

00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:45.016
- [Zach] Calvary guy.

00:00:45.016 --> 00:00:46.246
- Whoa, yes.
- Come on bro.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:47.550
(background laughter)

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:48.630
- Yeah, hence the tattoos and stuff.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.320
I think my strategy is gonna be this idea

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.200
of like really trying to
just hear the heart behind,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.360
like why people are pastoring.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.846
- Daniel, also youth
pastor, non-denominational.

00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:02.040
- I'm Anna, I'm actually
non-denominational,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.690
kind of all over the place over the years.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.030
- I mean I have question like, education?

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.000
So Seminary, maybe degrees, I don't know.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.830
- Seminary, Theological Studies.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.663
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:01:11.663 --> 00:01:12.496
- Where at?

00:01:12.496 --> 00:01:14.820
- Kings College as well
as Oxford University.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:15.653
- Oh, okay, yeah.

00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:17.130
- Was that like an MA or was it a MBA?

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.500
- You're drilling me in, aren't you?

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.317
- I know.
(group laughing)

00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:21.479
- I'm absolutely drilling you in.

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:22.312
- He's coming for you, dude.

00:01:22.312 --> 00:01:23.145
- Henry, your turn, your turn.

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:23.978
- Oh, my turn?

00:01:23.978 --> 00:01:25.051
- Your turn, yeah, man.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:25.884
- So give it to him.

00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:28.560
- So I'm in a MA program at
Western Seminary currently.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.030
- I went to Calvary Temple Bible college.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:30.863
- Come on dude.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.360
- And then did the Veritas program.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.760
So degree in Theology with
a focus in Biblical Studies.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.630
- Oh sweet
- Yeah.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.820
- Actually not really
a formal institution,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.830
but I grew up in such a rural area,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.420
there was just nothing for so far.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.470
So more of, almost more

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.860
of like an apprenticeship kind of context.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:45.693
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:45.693 --> 00:01:47.550
- Tiny town with tinier towns around it.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.410
So we all would kind of come to the same

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.020
Lutheran church primarily
when I was studying.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.880
- What kind of Lutheran church?

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.011
- Again, such a small
area, that just Lutheran.

00:01:57.011 --> 00:01:57.844
(laughing)

00:01:57.844 --> 00:01:58.677
There's so few people to get-

00:01:58.677 --> 00:01:59.510
- Generic brand.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:00.420
- Yes, generic.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.197
- [Don] Generic.
- Run of the mill Lutheran.

00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:03.600
- Gotcha.
- I like that.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.777
- Immediately I thought,

00:02:04.777 --> 00:02:06.510
"Well, if she's a Lutheran pastor

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.450
she would definitely say which
denomination she would be."

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.283
- Sorry.

00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:11.310
- What town did you grow up in?

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.720
- [Anna] Upper Michigan actually.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.560
Very small area.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.522
- What town?

00:02:14.522 --> 00:02:16.380
- Near Marquette, is kind of one

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.000
of the big cities around there, but

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:18.833
- Gotcha.

00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:21.510
- It's, it's the one
that someone would know.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.240
- Anna spoke in very general terms.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.880
I don't even think she knew the city

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.590
that she came from, but she knew the city

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.270
that was next to the city she came from?

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:31.560
And at that point I was done.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.430
- You mentioned that you didn't grow up

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:33.263
in the church?
- [Daniel] Right.

00:02:33.263 --> 00:02:34.560
- More of that going around?
- [Zach] Sure.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:35.393
- Okay, totally.

00:02:35.393 --> 00:02:36.780
- So I've been there all
my life, was baptized

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:37.830
at seven years old.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.330
I don't think I really became a believer

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.940
until I was 30 to be perfectly honest.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:41.773
- Okay.

00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:42.606
- I did it

00:02:42.606 --> 00:02:43.439
because I was brought up

00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:44.272
and this is what you're supposed to do.

00:02:44.272 --> 00:02:45.750
You do what they do and what they say

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:46.860
and you believe blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:47.820
So, yeah.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.241
And then I got kicked out.

00:02:49.241 --> 00:02:50.074
(record scratching)

00:02:50.074 --> 00:02:52.280
(everyone laughs)

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.612
(bell rings)

00:02:53.612 --> 00:02:55.319
(upbeat music)
(computer clicks)

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:56.631
(computer clicks)

00:02:56.631 --> 00:02:58.279
(computer clicks)

00:02:58.279 --> 00:02:59.112
(computer clicks)

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:00.600
- From what I knew about Lutheran's,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.730
there should have been
a very specific answer

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.949
to that question and that
kind of threw me off.

00:03:04.949 --> 00:03:06.112
(computer clicks)

00:03:06.112 --> 00:03:07.401
(computer clicks)

00:03:07.401 --> 00:03:09.457
(computer clicks)
- A-N-N-A. Done

00:03:09.457 --> 00:03:10.290
(Don laughs)

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.123
(buzzer buzzes)

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:12.480
- Of course the woman's not a pastor.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.339
- So that's-
- Check your bias.

00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:16.676
- Listen I . . . .

00:03:16.676 --> 00:03:17.509
- Bye.

00:03:17.509 --> 00:03:18.342
(Anna laughs)

00:03:18.342 --> 00:03:19.175
- Sorry, dude.

00:03:19.175 --> 00:03:20.008
- Being the only woman in the group today

00:03:20.008 --> 00:03:21.180
was a little tricky.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.700
Obviously not being able
to always edge my way

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.500
into the conversation, was not able

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.360
to present it in the way
that I would've liked

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.190
and that I thought would've
been more effective.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.230
- I walked in and I was so happy

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.850
that there was a female pastor

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.800
because they're the only types of people

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.050
that are as rare as gay
pastors like myself.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.420
It's very much still new

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.930
where we're seeing queer pastors
and queer worship leaders

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.340
and queer Christians.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.973
- Tell me. You got kicked out for what?

00:03:45.973 --> 00:03:46.806
- I got kicked out.

00:03:46.806 --> 00:03:48.660
Well, I got kicked out
because I was in the church.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.520
And as I grew older in the church,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.167
I began to ask questions.

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:54.093
That to me were very obvious.
- Yeah.

00:03:54.093 --> 00:03:55.920
- What was one of those questions?

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.510
- Is baptism essential for salvation?

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.343
- Yeah.

00:03:58.343 --> 00:04:01.530
- You know, communion some of
those battleground questions

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.680
that oftentimes separate
faiths and denominations yeah.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.480
- I pastored high school kids for years

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.850
and I never had any of them
asking me that question.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.580
- I want to know what everybody's
ministry responsibilities

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.500
are and what you're
currently teaching through.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.780
- Yeah, so I'm an
assistant teaching pastor,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.550
so we're replanting, revitalizing.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.260
So I'm an elder and a teaching pastor.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.470
- I serve at a small Korean
church in La Mirada, California

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.000
and I take care of the youth ministry.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.430
- So I'm kind of semi-retired.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.580
I also don't have a congregation,
although I work with one.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.980
It's an little Episcopal
church in San Clemente.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.800
I also have a weird job

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.760
that I'm chaplain to
a governmental agency.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.030
- Jay answered the question
about being retired,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.850
but he never indicated
what he was retired from.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.210
And after years of being
in ministry or serving,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.978
I thought there would've
been a culmination

00:04:49.978 --> 00:04:51.720
of his life experiences.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.480
And now he's doing a
part-time assistant thing?

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:55.447
And I thought,

00:04:55.447 --> 00:04:57.870
"Okay, so we've slipped
into some unknown factors

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:58.860
about yourself, Jay."

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.060
- I don't pastor a church.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.913
I pastor pastors.

00:05:01.913 --> 00:05:03.480
- Oh, lovely.
- So I work for a denomination

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.990
and I preach at different
congregations every week.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:07.823
- Wow.

00:05:07.823 --> 00:05:08.850
- I'm not in the pulpit right now,

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.820
but I am teaching in small
groups, conversations.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.650
There's a lot of dogmatic positions

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.110
that are just generalized
for Christiandom.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:16.943
- So I have a question.

00:05:16.943 --> 00:05:19.920
I mean, when was the
last time like you were

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.420
in a hospital room with somebody

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.100
and what comfort did you give them

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.660
in a time that was critical?

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.493
- Oh, I hate this question.

00:05:25.493 --> 00:05:28.560
- I think that someone who
is in pastoral ministry

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.720
it's more than just
preaching and teaching.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.120
Well, I felt a question
that would get at that

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.130
about how somebody gives the comfort

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.980
and the peace of the presence
of God would really indicate

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.570
whether or not they were a pastor

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.550
and they were involved
in that type of ministry.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.190
Last time I was in the
hospital was not too long ago.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.720
It was, I'd actually become almost a

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.855
medical proxy for a
family friend's mother.

00:05:48.855 --> 00:05:50.727
She was elderly and just
kind of going through it.

00:05:50.727 --> 00:05:52.890
And it was very, very tender for me.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.080
But the comfort that I gave was knowing

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.610
that her family was nearby.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.830
And knowing that beyond this moment

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.140
when she stepped out of time into eternity

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.270
she would be in the
place she longed to be.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.290
- I've only had to do it once.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:05.130
And it was very recent

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.140
and I hated it with
every fiber of my being,

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.270
because it was for
somebody that like I loved.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:14.100
And it was a lot of waiting and quiet

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.170
and like very, very soft prayers

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.850
and just kind of being there.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.482
And I'm shaking. I hated it.

00:06:19.482 --> 00:06:20.373
(bell ringing)

00:06:20.373 --> 00:06:21.206
(air whooshing)

00:06:21.206 --> 00:06:22.521
(upbeat music)
(computer clicks)

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:23.354
(computer clicks)

00:06:23.354 --> 00:06:24.420
- The thing that I thought I spotted was

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.960
that he was just trying too hard.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:27.132
(computer clicks)

00:06:27.132 --> 00:06:27.970
(computer clicks)

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:28.803
(computer clicks)

00:06:28.803 --> 00:06:30.450
- The way that he was
answering the questions

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.613
was very old school.

00:06:32.613 --> 00:06:33.582
(computer clicks)

00:06:33.582 --> 00:06:34.791
(buzzer buzzes)

00:06:34.791 --> 00:06:37.242
- Ah, do y'all know Jesus?
- Sorry brother.

00:06:37.242 --> 00:06:38.075
(All contestants laugh)

00:06:38.075 --> 00:06:38.908
- Sorry dude.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.160
- I see what's happening.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.149
First the woman, then the
black man, the gay is next.

00:06:43.149 --> 00:06:44.250
(chuckles)

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.270
I see what's happening.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.220
- What the devil? Me?

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.470
I question everybody else left up there.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.977
Do they know Jesus? 'Cause I know Him.

00:06:51.977 --> 00:06:52.810
(air rumbling)

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.930
- [Announcer] If you think
the Mole is still in the box

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.000
and you wanna continue playing the game,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.203
please raise your hand.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.010
- So let's start with the question of

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.870
what does atheism mean to you?

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.880
- I talk to my kids a
lot about agnosticism.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:06.713
- Yeah.

00:07:06.713 --> 00:07:07.560
- More than I talk about atheism.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.393
- Yeah. Right.

00:07:08.393 --> 00:07:09.226
- Just, you know, because of the

00:07:09.226 --> 00:07:10.387
oversimplification of like, as an atheist,

00:07:10.387 --> 00:07:11.737
"I don't believe in God." Well it's like,

00:07:11.737 --> 00:07:12.570
"Well what is God?"

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.460
- I go back to like what
Augustine of Hippo said.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.040
Like "All hearts are restless
until they find rest in God."

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.260
An atheist is not the enemy. It's not.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.610
Just because you don't believe in the God

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:21.990
that I love and serve.
- Correct.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:22.980
- Doesn't mean you're the enemy.

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.200
It means that you're
restless in your heart

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.420
and you're trying to search
for meaning and belonging

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.700
in anything other than God.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.560
- I'd be curious to know anybody on stage

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:34.320
that did not have a real
serious questioning time?

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.430
- You, you start.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.034
- [Zach] Yeah go for it.
- You are very quiet.

00:07:37.034 --> 00:07:37.867
(everyone laughs)

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:38.700
And that just a little sus to me.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:39.533
- [Zach] What was the question?

00:07:39.533 --> 00:07:40.366
You are the best dressed so.

00:07:40.366 --> 00:07:41.199
(laughing)

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:42.090
- I'm a little overdressed today.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.070
- So the question was, have
I ever wrestled with faith?

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:44.903
To be honest,

00:07:44.903 --> 00:07:46.590
I wrestle more with my
calling more than my faith.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.690
My faith, once I had an
experience and encounter with God

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.850
out of drug addiction, that,

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:54.720
my faith was pretty much, pretty solid.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.650
But with the calling,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.420
I think I wrestled
countless amount of times,

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.820
In high school, my
parents decided to split

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:01.470
and that's when I really went on a frenzy

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.520
with drug addiction.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.790
I struggled with meth, crystal
meth from my junior year.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:09.990
I think that's what really
put me to the pit of my life,

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.000
the most rock bottom of my life.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.270
And the only way I could go is to look up.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.103
- So I have a question.

00:08:16.103 --> 00:08:17.430
How do we feel about eschatology?

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.750
- Well we're a Millennial.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:19.740
- You're all millennial? Okay.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:20.880
- Very cool.
- So, okay.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.980
Yeah, we are in the end times.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.600
We were the second Jesus
said it was finished.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.360
- Yeah I have a lot more openness

